\section*{Existence of the Smith–Toda Complex \$V(4)\$}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Manuscript 1 (family 308, 2026-09-23): The paper claims an ordinary finite Smith--Toda complex V(4) specifically at prime 1009, with BP homology quotient by the first powers of 1009 and v\_1 through v\_4. Manuscript 2 (family 308, 2026-09-23): At every finite chromatic height n, some prime is claimed to admit an ordinary finite Smith--Toda spectrum realizing BP\_*/(p,v\_1,...,v\_n), with first powers and the canonical comodule structure.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} At the specified prime $p=1009$, OpenAI claims a finite $p$-local spectrum $X$ with
\[
BP_*X\simeq BP_*/(p,v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4)
\]
as graded $BP_*BP$-comodules, with the quotient coaction specified canonically, with the generating class placed in degree zero. Here $BP$ is Brown--Peterson homology and $v_i$ the usual generators. This realizes first powers in a Smith--Toda $V(4)$, without a ring-spectrum assertion.

\paragraph{Mathematical claim 2.} For each integer $n\ge0$, OpenAI claims the existence of a finite $p$-local spectrum $X$ at some prime $p$ satisfying
\[
BP_*X\simeq BP_*/(p,v_1,\ldots,v_n)
\]
as graded Brown--Peterson comodules with canonical quotient coaction and generator of degree zero. Finite means a retract of a localized finite CW spectrum. The prime may depend on $n$, all displayed exponents are one, and no bound on the prime or multiplicative spectrum structure is claimed.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 308, 2026-09-23): Finite cofibers first produce V(3) with a binary product and two-sided unit, sufficient to realize a sphere class as a self-map. Filtered cobar estimates enumerate the possible differentials on v\_4. Cyclic extended powers and a two-cell averaging retract kill the remaining beta-power targets early enough in the spectral sequence, allowing the realizing cofiber to be formed.

Manuscript 2 (family 308, 2026-09-23): An ultraproduct of ordinary prime-local spectra retains the varying sphere degrees during finite Koszul cofiber constructions. Affine permutation powers and one Euler annihilator make the comparison with cofibers exact after localization, producing the next actual sphere class. Cobordism operations identify its first-power effect on BP homology, and the finite maps and homotopies descend to an individual prime.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 308, 2026-09-23): Theorem 1.1 supplies the requested finite V(4), with precisely BP*/(p,v1,v2,v3,v4) and canonical quotient comodule, at the explicit prime 1009.

Manuscript 2 (family 308, 2026-09-23): Theorem 1.1 constructs a finite first-power Smith–Toda V(n) for every n at some prime, so n=4 answers the entire any-prime V(4) existence question with a stronger all-height result.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 308 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

